// The first slice in this frame cut from a FINDING rather than from the storm.
//
// Eighteen slices came out of ES.260819.01 in one sitting. This one comes out of
// ITER.260821.17/AG1, which found a correct rule reporting nothing because its refusals
// were computed and silently dropped — caught only because the answer had been predicted.
// Three attribution bugs, each found by a rule firing and never by a test.

thin-slice "TS.260821.02" {
    slug "prove-every-rule-can-refuse"
    title "Report any declared rule that nothing in the record demonstrates refusing"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.delivery-record"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "prove-rules"
    actor "automation"
    produces-event "RuleWitnessed" "RuleUnwitnessed"
    needs-read-model "what-is-currently-true"

    trigger "the record declares a rule, and nobody knows whether it can fire"
    outcome "every declared rule is either witnessed by a refusal the engine actually computed, or named as unwitnessed"

    scenario #"""
        Given a record declaring rules, some enforced and some only written down
        When the rules are proved
        Then each is exercised against a record built to violate it, and any rule that refuses
             nothing is REPORTED by name — because a rule that has never been shown to refuse
             is indistinguishable from one that cannot, and that is S3 turned on the
             enforcement layer itself.
        """#

    step "1" does="read every rule the record declares. The set comes from the record, never from the engine"
    step "2" does="exercise each against a witness — a record built to violate exactly that rule"
    step "3" does="a rule whose witness produces no refusal is reported, naming the rule"
    step "4" does="a rule the engine carries no witness for is reported too, and differently — unwitnessed is not the same as unenforceable"

    excludes "judging whether a rule is a GOOD rule. It proves the rule can fire, never that it should exist"
    excludes "generating witnesses. A witness is written by whoever declares the rule, which is what \
        makes it evidence rather than a second implementation of the same check"
    excludes "refusing on an unwitnessed rule. A rule declared today for a shape the record does not \
        hold yet is legitimate — what is not legitimate is nobody knowing which"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="the skill teaches that declaring a rule includes writing the record that violates it"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a-rule-has-a-witness, reported over the whole declared rule set"
    layer "docs"        reaches="the unwitnessed set is readable, so `which of our gates has never fired` stops being unanswerable"

    claim "C1" text="a declared rule that no witness makes refuse is reported, naming the rule" \
        settled-by="a test declaring a rule with a witness that does not violate it, asserting the rule is named"
    claim "C2" text="a witness proves the rule by producing a refusal the engine computed, never by asserting one exists" \
        settled-by="a test asserting the report is derived from running the checker over the witness, and that a witness claiming a refusal it does not produce is itself reported"
    claim "C3" text="a rule whose refusals are computed and then dropped has no witness" \
        settled-by="a test reintroducing AG1 — a refusal about a nested entity — and asserting the rule loses its witness"
    claim "C4" text="an-immutable-record-carries-a-seal gains a witness, and sealing this record settles what it refuses" \
        settled-by="a witness for the seal rule, plus the accepted decisions and resolved symptoms of this record sealed"

    depends-on "TS.260821.01 — a rule is declared on the schema before it can be witnessed"

    useful-alone "`which of our gates has never fired` becomes answerable. Worth having with nothing \
        else changed, because the answer today is that nobody knows"

    attacks "S3"

    cut-from "ITER.260821.17/AG1" \
        note="cut from a finding rather than from the storm. Whether this reaches a release is what \
            decides the fitness function a-finding-travelled-the-whole-loop, which has never fired"

    principle-note "a rule that cannot be shown to refuse is an assertion about enforcement, and \
        this frame exists because assertions about fidelity are what nobody can check. Building an \
        engine that computes refusals does not escape it — it moves it one level down, to whether \
        the computation runs at all"

    proves "every declared rule either refuses something or is named as having refused nothing"
    does-not-prove "that the rule refuses the RIGHT things. A witness shows a rule can fire; \
        whether it fires on what it should is what its own tests are for"
}
